# VT52 mode: DECANM reset (CSI ? 2 l) makes the terminal read the VT52's escape sequences alone and
# ESC < brings ANSI mode back. The VT100 User Guide's "Valid VT52 Mode Control Sequences" and xterm's
# ctlseqs ("VT52 Mode") are the texts; the VT52 Maintenance Manual (chapter 2) has the acceptance
# tests for the direct addressing.

test DECANM reset enters VT52 mode and ESC < leaves it
spec VT100 User Guide, DECANM: the reset state interprets only VT52 sequences, the set state only ANSI ones; xterm ctlseqs: ESC < exits VT52 mode
size 10 4
feed \e[?2l\e[2;3Hx
screen
|2;3Hx|
feed \e<\e[2;3Hy
screen
|2;3Hx|
|  y|

test the cursor keys' sequences move the cursor and stop at the margins
spec VT100 User Guide, VT52 mode: ESC A, B, C and D move one position and stop at the margins; xterm ctlseqs: ESC A cursor up, ESC B down, ESC C right, ESC D left
size 5 3
feed \e[2;3H\e[?2l\eA\eA\eA\eD\eD\eD\eD
cursor 1,1
feed \eB\eB\eB\eC\eC\eC\eC\eC\eC
cursor 3,5

test ESC H homes the cursor and ESC J and ESC K erase
spec VT100 User Guide, VT52 mode: ESC H cursor to home, ESC J erase to end of screen, ESC K erase to end of line; xterm ctlseqs VT52 Mode
size 5 3
feed aaaaa\r\nbbbbb\r\nccccc\e[?2l\eH\eC\eC\eK
screen
|aa|
|bbbbb|
|ccccc|
feed \eB\eJ
screen
|aa|
|bb|

test ESC I is a reverse line feed that scrolls down at the top
spec VT100 User Guide, VT52 mode: ESC I moves up one position, a scroll down at the top margin
size 5 3
feed a\r\nb\e[?2l\eH\eI
screen
||
|a|
|b|
cursor 1,1

test ESC Y addresses the cursor with row and column, each the number plus 32
spec VT100 User Guide, VT52 mode: ESC Y line column, the numbers sent as ASCII codes whose values are the number plus 0378 (32); xterm ctlseqs: the screen coordinate system is 0-based
size 20 6
feed \e[?2l\eY#'X
row 4 |       X|
cursor 4,9

test ESC Y reaches the corners of a 24 by 80 screen
spec VT52 Maintenance Manual 2.4.6: ESC Y 7 o is the last position of the last line, ESC Y 7 SPACE its first, ESC Y SPACE o the last of the first line
size 80 24
feed \e[?2l\eY7\sa\eY7ob\eY\soc\eY\s\sd
cell 24,1 text=a
cell 24,80 text=b
cell 1,80 text=c
cell 1,1 text=d

test ESC Y survives its two characters arriving apart
spec VT100 User Guide, VT52 mode: ESC Y line column
size 20 6
feed \e[?2l\eY
feed #
feed 'X
row 4 |       X|

test an address past the screen leaves that axis where it was
convention a coordinate the screen does not have is no move on that axis (the vttest VT52 test draws with such addresses and expects the column alone to move)
size 10 4
feed \e[?2l\eY!!
cursor 2,2
feed \eY~%
cursor 2,6

test ESC Z identifies a VT52 emulated by a VT100
spec xterm ctlseqs VT52 Mode: ESC Z is answered ESC / Z ("I am a VT52 emulated by VT100"); VT100 User Guide: Identify, ESC / Z
size 10 4
feed \e[?2l\eZ
reply \e/Z

test graphics mode draws line characters until ESC G
spec VT100 User Guide, VT52 mode: ESC F selects a special graphics character set, ESC G the ASCII set (the VT100's graphics stand in for the VT52's)
size 10 4
feed \e[?2l\eFlqk\eGlqk
screen
|┌─┐lqk|

test there is no wraparound in VT52 mode
spec VT100 User Guide, DECAWM: the reset state replaces the character at the right margin; the VT52 has no autowrap
size 5 3
feed \e[?2labcdefg
screen
|abcdg|
cursor 1,5

test ANSI mode's settings come back with ESC <
convention VT52 mode suspends the modes that do not exist on a VT52 (autowrap, origin, insert, new line) and the character sets, and ESC < restores them (xterm does the same)
size 5 3
feed \e(0\e[?2lq\e<\e[1;1H\e[2Kq
screen
|─|
feed \e[1;5H\e(Bab
screen
|─   a|
|b|

test CSI is not read in VT52 mode
spec VT100 User Guide, DECANM: only VT52 compatible escape sequences are interpreted while DECANM is reset
size 10 4
feed \e[?2l\e[1;31mx
screen
|1;31mx|

test ESC < enters VT100 mode
spec VT510 DECANM, Table 5-1: ESC < is "Exit VT52 mode. Enter VT100 mode."; xterm sets its VT100 level on it, and DECRQM (a VT300 function) is then not answered
size 10 4
feed \e[?2l\e<\e[c
reply \e[?1;2c
feed \e[?7$p
reply none

test the VT52's printer controls are ignored
spec VT100 User Guide DECANM: ESC ], V, W, X, ^ and _ are the printer and autoprint controls of VT52 mode, which this terminal has no printer for
size 10 4
feed \e[?2l\e]\eV\eW\eX\e^\e_ab
screen
|ab|

test a full reset leaves VT52 mode
convention RIS is not a VT52 sequence, but a program that dies in VT52 mode would leave a terminal that reset cannot bring back
size 10 4
feed \e[?2l\ec\e[1;3Hx
screen
|  x|

test text and controls act as in ANSI mode
spec VT52 Maintenance Manual 2.4.2: CTRL H moves the cursor left, CTRL I to the next tab stop, CTRL J down, CTRL M to the left-hand position
size 12 3
feed \e[?2lab\bc\tx\r\nhi
screen
|ac      x|
|hi|

test DECRQM answers DECANM as set in ANSI mode
spec VT510 DECRQM and xterm ctlseqs: CSI ? 2 $ p; the terminal that can answer is in ANSI mode
size 10 4
feed \e[?2$p
reply \e[?2;1$y
